  "slug": "classification-single-gene-set-2d4b",
  "summary": "Seven preprints apply transformers, autoencoders and equivariant networks to cell embeddings, spatial inference and population discovery across modalities.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learning cell-state representations with deep models across scales and data types. Foundation-style models include scUNVEIL, pretrained on about 92 million human cells with next-UMI prediction, and Bloom, a transformer trained on 300,000 mouse brain cells that unifies expression and chromatin accessibility. Spatial work either resolves subcellular organization, using a rotation-equivariant network on 3D transcript embeddings, or deconvolves multicellular spots into single-cell profiles with st2sc. Other items address cohort structure, with scPACE modeling donor and cell states and imputing missing cell types, and de novo population discovery by combining clustering with fine-tuned Geneformer outputs. One preprint extends the theme to imaging, predicting cell movement from images to improve LAP-based tracking by 2.7 to 5.7 percentage points.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
